[Event "Vienna: the set up"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/9NYe5QGB"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Time to learn the Vienna: a quiet knight first, and then the f pawn flies. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { The king pawn takes the middle. So far, nothing unusual. } (1. d4 { A great opening, and a completely different day out. } 1... d5 2. c4 e6 { That is the queens gambit. Fine chess, but the Vienna needs the king pawn. }) (1. Nf3 { Also excellent. But it keeps your plans hidden for a while yet. } 1... d5 2. d4 Nf6 { A quiet queen pawn game. We want the king pawn out first. }) 1... e5 { He answers in kind. Now the Vienna move: the queen knight before anything else. [%cal Yb1c3] } 2. Nc3 { This guards e4 and keeps the f pawn free to charge later. That is the whole idea. [%csl Gc3] } (2. Nf3 { The most popular move in chess, and a very good one. } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 { That is the Spanish. Excellent chess, but today we play the Vienna. }) (2. Bc4 { The bishop first. It often becomes the same thing anyway. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Bc5 { You have transposed back to our line. We play the knight first. }) (2. d4 { The centre game. You rip the middle open, but you give up a pawn. } 2... exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight hits your queen and you hand the time straight back. }) (2. f4 { The kings gambit. The right idea, but two whole moves too early. } 2... exf4 3. Nf3 g5 { He keeps the pawn and your king feels the draught. Knight first. }) 2... Nc6 { He develops. Now the bishop, pointing straight at his weakest pawn. [%cal Yf1c4] } 3. Bc4 { On c4 the bishop stares at f7 for the rest of the game. [%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Nf3 { Also strong, and very natural too. } 3... Nf6 4. d4 exd4 { A fine open game. But the bishop first keeps your f pawn free. }) (3. f4 { The gambit, but too soon: his knight is on c6, not f6. } 3... exf4 4. Nf3 g5 { He simply keeps it. The gambit needs his knight on f6 first. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna. You fianchetto and play a slow, solid game. } 3... a5 4. Bg2 Bc5 { Perfectly good chess. But we came here for the bishop on c4. }) (3. Bb5 { The Spanish bishop. But there is no pin here, and it can be kicked. } 3... Nd4 4. Nf3 c6 { The knight jumps out and your bishop must come back to c4 anyway. }) 3... Nf6 { His knight eyes e4. Do not defend with a piece; a little pawn does it. [%cal Yd2d3] } 4. d3 { Solid. And now every one of his pieces has to respect f4 coming. [%csl Gd3] } (4. Nf3 { Natural, but count again: what is guarding your king pawn? } 4... Nxe4 5. Nxe4 d5 { The fork trick. He gives the knight and takes it straight back. Level. }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never this. One check is not worth a whole bishop. } 4... Kxf7 { He simply takes, walks his king home, and you are a piece down for nothing. }) (4. Bb3 { You tuck the bishop away before anyone has even asked it to move. } 4... Bc5 5. d3 a5 { Playable, but you spent a move on nothing and his pawn comes at you. }) (4. Bb5 { The Spanish idea again, and again the knight answers it. } 4... Nd4 5. Nf3 c6 { The knight jumps in and the bishop is chased. c4 was the square. }) 4... Bc5 { His bishop settles. This is the moment: throw the f pawn. [%cal Yf2f4] } 5. f4 { A kings gambit two moves late, with everything defended. This is the Vienna soul. [%cal Gf4e5] } (5. Nf3 { Calm, sound, and a very respectable choice. } 5... O-O 6. O-O h6 { A pleasant game with a tiny edge. But the f pawn is why we are here. }) (5. h3 { A useful little move, stopping his bishop coming to g4. } 5... Na5 6. Qf3 c6 { Perfectly good, and perfectly quiet. We choose the storm instead. }) (5. Bg5 { Pin the knight first. This is what the books play most often. } 5... h6 6. Bh4 Be7 { Also fine chess. But our Vienna throws the f pawn at him. }) (5. a4 { A quiet wing move, stopping his pawn coming to a5 later on. } 5... d6 6. Nf3 h6 { Nothing wrong with it, and nothing exciting about it either. }) (5. Nd5 { The knight jumps into the hole. But he can trade it off at once. } 5... Nxd5 6. Bxd5 Qh4 { Level, and now his queen is the busy one. The f pawn is better. }) 5... d6 { He holds the centre and keeps the tension. Finish developing: the king knight. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Nf3 { Every minor piece is out: knights c3 and f3, bishop c4, pawns d3 and f4 backing the centre. His king still has to pick a side. [%csl Gf3] } (6. f5 { The clamp. Just as good, and you will meet it in a later chapter. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 c6 { Equal and very playable. Here we finish developing first. }) (6. fxe5 { Releasing the tension. But look what square you open for his knight. } 6... Ng4 7. exd6 Qxd6 { His knight lands on g4 and suddenly you are the one defending. }) (6. Nd5 { The jump again, and again he simply takes it off. } 6... Nxd5 7. Bxd5 exf4 { Your best knight is gone and he keeps the pawn. You are worse. }) (6. h3 { A quiet move at exactly the wrong moment. } 6... O-O 7. Nf3 a5 { He castles into safety while you fiddle. Half a pawn for nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he takes on f4"]
