[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The Vienna set up is on the board. This time he is about to take your f pawn at once. }
5... exf4 { He has grabbed it. How do you win the pawn back without loosening your king? } 6. Bxf4 { The bishop, and it lands on a working square with nothing hanging. [%csl Gf4] } (6. Nf3 { Developing instead of taking. Just as good by the numbers. } 6... Ng4 7. Bxf4 Nf2 { His knight forks your queen and rook. Still level, but what a mess. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (6. Bd2 { The long way round to f4. But it hands him a free move. } 6... Nd4 7. Bxf4 c6 { He has planted a knight in the middle while you walked. You are worse. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. h3 { A quiet pawn move with your king still standing in the centre. } 6... d5 7. exd5 Nd4 { He rips the middle open and you are the one in trouble. [%csl Rd4] }) 6... d6 { He props the centre up. Now bring out the last piece. } 7. Nf3 { Nice, that is the one. Everything is out and the f file is yours. [%csl Gf3] } (7. Qd2 { The queen steps up, but your knight is still asleep at home. } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 Be6 { He is castled and comfortable. Level, and you spent a move. }) (7. Bb3 { Tucking the bishop away before anyone has asked it to move. } 7... Ng4 8. Qd2 Nf2 { Level, but you handed him that fork for nothing. [%cal Rf2d1] }) (7. Bg5 { A pin, except his knight is not stuck to anything at all. } 7... h6 8. Bh4 O-O { He kicks it and castles. Nothing gained. }) 7... O-O { He tucks his king away, finally caught up in development. } 8. d4 { The pawn comes back to the middle and hits his bishop. You stand freer everywhere. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4c5] } (8. Qd2 { Also fine, and the queen is useful on that square. } 8... Be6 9. Bxe6 fxe6 { Level. But the pawn to d4 hits his bishop and wins time. }) (8. Bb3 { The bishop steps back with nobody chasing it. } 8... a5 9. Qd2 Be6 { He grabs space on the wing while you shuffle. }) (8. Na4 { Hunting the bishop, but your knight leaves the middle to do it. } 8... d5 9. Nxc5 dxc4 { He opens the centre and your knight is far from home. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he castles early"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/YTC07ILj"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ Same Vienna set up. This time he is about to tuck his king away instead of taking. Perfect: you already know where to aim. }
5... O-O { The king has picked its corner. Do not storm yet. What is still at home? } 6. Nf3 { Well spotted. Finish developing first; the pawn storm keeps. [%csl Gf3] } (6. f5 { The clamp, one move too early. Your last piece is still in bed. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 b5 { His knight jumps to the hole and you have no time to chase it. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. fxe5 { Releasing the tension. Now watch which piece gets the free square. } 6... d5 7. exf6 Qxf6 { His queen comes out with the pawn back and you are worse. }) (6. Bb3 { Stepping the bishop away before anybody chases it. } 6... d6 7. f5 Nd4 { A wasted move, and his knight takes the hole. You are worse. }) (6. Bd5 { Planting the bishop in the middle where his knight can hit it. } 6... exf4 7. Bxf4 Nd4 { He is a pawn up for a moment and his knight is superb. }) 6... d6 { He holds the middle. Now the pawn may run past. } 7. f5 { Yes. The clamp fixes his king side and the g pawn comes next. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gg2g4] } (7. Na4 { Hunting the bishop first. Also perfectly good. } 7... Bb6 8. Nxb6 axb6 { Equal and quiet. The clamp is far more ambitious. }) (7. fxe5 { You let the tension go and his pieces breathe. } 7... dxe5 8. Na4 Bb4+ { A check, and now you are the one solving problems. }) (7. h3 { Too slow. He takes on f4 at the moment that suits him. } 7... exf4 8. Bxf4 Be6 { He trades off your good bishop and stands well. }) 7... Nd4 { His knight jumps in. Yours can jump further. } 8. Nd5 { Trades favour the attacker here, and d5 is the better hole. [%csl Gd5] } (8. Nxd4 { Trading first. It is fine, just less ambitious. } 8... exd4 9. Nd5 Nxd5 { Equal and simplified. The knight to d5 first keeps more pieces on. }) (8. Bg5 { A pin, but you have left his knight sitting in the middle. } 8... c6 9. Na4 Bb4+ { A check again, and his knight still owns d4. }) (8. a4 { A wing move while a knight sits in your half. } 8... c6 9. Nxd4 exd4 { You had to take anyway, a move later and worse. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the knight hunts your bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
