[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ If he copies your Vienna bishop instead of bringing the knight out, copying has a price. }
3... Bc5 { He mirrors you, so his king knight is still at home. What is unguarded? } 4. Qg4 { Out she comes, straight at g7, and he has no comfortable answer. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Qh5 { The sister jump, and just as good. } 4... Qf6 5. Nf3 Nge7 { Two roads, the same headache for him. Take either. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet, respectable development. Nothing wrong with it. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O O-O { A normal game. But the queen asks him hard questions right now. }) (4. d3 { Solid and sound, and it keeps everything defended. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 h6 { A pleasant edge. The queen sortie is sharper. }) (4. Nd5 { The knight jumps at the hole, but nothing is attacking it. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 O-O { He develops with tempo and you have gained nothing. }) 4... Kf8 { His best try: give up castling to save the pawn. Now put her somewhere safe. } 5. Qf3 { She slides out of danger and aims at f7 again. [%cal Gf3f7] } (5. Qxg7+ { Look again: his king walked to f8, and f8 guards g7. } 5... Kxg7 { The queen is simply eaten. That is the game. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (5. Qg3 { Also good, and just as pointed. } 5... Nf6 6. Nge2 h5 { Equally pleasant. Both squares keep her active. }) (5. Qh5 { She stays out, but she can be chased again from there. } 5... Qf6 6. Nf3 g6 { The pawn hits her and you lose the time you won. }) (5. Qd1 { All the way home. Safe, and completely pointless. } 5... Qh4 6. Qf3 Nf6 { He has the initiative now, and his king is fine. }) 5... Nf6 { He develops at last. Bring the knight out behind her. } 6. Nge2 { The king knight, keeping f3 for the queen. Everything holds. [%csl Ge2] } (6. d3 { Solid, but it lets his knight into the middle for free. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 c6 { His knight owns d4 and your queen has been chased home. }) (6. Qd1 { Retreating with no reason. He is suddenly the one attacking. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 c6 { You have lost three moves with the queen for nothing. }) (6. Bb3 { The bishop steps back while your king is still uncastled. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 d5 { He takes the middle and you are worse. }) 6... Nd4 { The knight jumps at your queen. Trade it before it settles. } 7. Nxd4 { Take it off. Anything else and that knight rules the board. [%csl Gd4] } (7. Qd3 { Stepping aside and letting the knight stay. } 7... a5 8. O-O c6 { His knight sits in the middle for the rest of the game. }) (7. Qg3 { Sidestepping, but count what his knight hits from d4. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks and eats your rook. You are lost. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Qxf6+ { A queen trade, but you gave the lady for a knight. } 7... Qxf6 8. Nd5 Qd8 { A whole queen for a knight. The game is over. [%csl Rf6] }) 7... exd4 { He takes back and his pawn hits your knight. } 8. Nd5 { Forward, not backward. The knight takes the hole his own pawn made. [%csl Gd5] } (8. Ne2 { Retreating. Safe, but the knight ends up doing nothing. } 8... Qe7 9. c3 Qxe4 { He wins the pawn back and stands well. }) (8. Na4 { The rim again. Your knight is a long way from his king. } 8... Bd6 9. c3 Rb8 { Level at best, and your knight is stranded. }) (8. e5 { A push that gives away the pawn you were guarding. } 8... dxc3 9. exf6 cxd2+ { His pawn eats yours with check. Nothing gained. }) 1-0


