[Role "exercise"]

{ The same Vienna copycat start. This time his queen is about to come out and play the hero. }
4... Qf6 { She guards g7. It looks clever. Where can you hit her and gain something? } 5. Nd5 { The knight hits the queen and eyes c7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5f6,Gd5c7] } (5. Qxg7 { Wait. His queen guards g7 now, and she is looking right at you. } 5... Qxg7 { You have handed over the lady for a pawn. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (5. Qg3 { A sensible retreat, and the game stays roughly level. } 5... Nge7 6. Nf3 Nd4 { Nothing wrong, but you missed something much better. }) (5. Qf3 { Offering the trade. But his queen is the badly placed one. } 5... Nd4 6. Qxf6 Nxf6 { You traded off your best chance. He is fine. }) (5. d4 { Opening lines while his queen still sits on f6. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nxd4 { He takes the pawn and you are worse. }) 5... Qxf2+ { She grabs a pawn with check. Let her come. She will not get home. } 6. Kd1 { The only square. Sidestep and smile. [%csl Gd1] } 6... Kf8 { He tucks the king away. Now look at that queen. How do you close the net? } 7. Nh3 { The knight covers her exits and attacks her right now — she has no comfortable square left. [%cal Gh3f2] } (7. Qxg7+ { Look again: his king is standing right beside that pawn. } 7... Kxg7 { The queen is gone and so is the game. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (7. Qf3 { Offering the trade lets her escape the net for free. } 7... Qxf3+ 8. Nxf3 Bd6 { Queens off, and the trap you built has vanished. }) (7. Qe2 { The same problem: you invite her to trade herself off. } 7... Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 Bd6 { Level. The queen walked out through the front door. }) (7. Nxc7 { A fork of the rook, but you let her go to catch it. } 7... d5 8. Qf3 dxc4 { He gives up the rook and grabs your bishop. You are worse. }) 1-0


