[Role "exercise"]

{ The same Vienna copycat idea. This time a knight is about to leap at you instead of guarding home. }
4... Nd4 { The knight jumps in and leaves g7 all alone. What is on offer? } 5. Qxg7 { The pawn is free, and his corner rook is suddenly very nervous. [%cal Gg7h8] } (5. Bxf7+ { A check, but you are giving a bishop for one pawn. } 5... Kxf7 { He takes and walks home. The free pawn was on g7. [%cal Re8f7] }) (5. Bb3 { Saving the bishop from the knight. Safe, and far too slow. } 5... Nxb3 6. axb3 Nf6 { He trades it off anyway and the free pawn has gone. }) (5. Nd5 { The other jump, but his knight is the one causing trouble. } 5... g6 6. Qd1 c6 { His pawn shuts your queen out and you have nothing. }) (5. Kd1 { Sidestepping a fork that has not happened yet. } 5... Kf8 6. Qh5 Qe8 { A wasted move, and g7 is guarded now. }) 5... Qf6 { His best try: offer queens to save the rook. } 6. Qxf6 { Take, and stay a clean pawn up. Anything greedier goes wrong. [%csl Gf6] } (6. Qxh8 { The rook looks free. But count her escape squares in that corner. } 6... Qxh8 { She is trapped and simply eaten. You gave a queen for a rook. [%cal Rf6h8] }) (6. Qg3 { Backing off with the queen and leaving the rook alone. } 6... Ne7 7. Nf3 Rg8 { He saves everything and you are worse. }) (6. Bxf7+ { A second bite at f7, and it costs a bishop again. } 6... Qxf7 7. Qxe5+ Ne6 { You won two pawns for a bishop and your king is bare. }) (6. Qxf7+ { The other capture with check, but it is guarded twice. } 6... Qxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 { Queen for queen and bishop, and you are the one down. }) 6... Nxf6 { He recaptures. Now finish developing. } 7. Bd3 { A pawn up for nothing, and the bishop guards c2. [%csl Gc2] } (7. Nf3 { Natural, but look what his knight on d4 is aiming at. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks king and rook, and the rook is gone. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Bb3 { Also playable, but c2 is still hanging in the air. } 7... Rg8 8. Kf1 c6 { Level-ish, and your king cannot castle. }) (7. Kd1 { Guarding c2 with the king. It works, but it is clumsy. } 7... b5 8. Bxb5 Nxb5 { You lose the bishop and the extra pawn with it. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit accepted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/X3YXFT9u"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Now his other defence: the king knight first. This is where the real gambit lives. }
2... Nf6 { The knight guards e5 and stares at your e pawn. Throw the f pawn at him. [%cal Yf2f4] } 3. f4 { The Vienna gambit: give up the f pawn to rip the position open before he is ready. Sound or not, over the board it is a monster. [%cal Gf4e5] } (3. Nf3 { The calm road, and a very solid choice. } 3... Nc6 4. d4 Bb4 { Solid and sound. But it is not why we chose the Vienna. }) (3. Bc4 { The bishop first, heading for f7. Perfectly good too. } 3... Bc5 4. Nge2 O-O { A quiet game. The gambit is the sharper weapon. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna, with a fianchetto to come. } 3... Bc5 4. Bg2 O-O { Playable and pleasant. Today we want the fireworks. }) (3. d4 { Straight down the middle, but he just takes. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight hits your queen and you have lost the time. }) 3... exf4 { He takes the bait. Now hit the knight before it settles. [%cal Ye4e5] } 4. e5 { The point of the whole gambit. The knight has nowhere good to go. [%cal Ge5f6] } (4. Bc4 { Developing instead of pushing. But now he consolidates. } 4... c6 5. d4 d5 { He keeps the pawn and builds a wall. Your gambit bought nothing. }) (4. Nf3 { Also natural, and also a move too slow. } 4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He keeps the pawn and still gets a fine game. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you left the knight alone. } 4... Bb4 5. Bd3 d5 { The pin arrives and your push to e5 has gone for good. }) 4... Ng8 { All the way home. He owns one cold pawn; you own the board. [%csl Rg8][%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { Develop with tempo while his pieces sit in bed. [%csl Gf3] } (5. Bc4 { The bishop is good there, but the knight is faster. } 5... d6 6. Qe2 dxe5 { He gives the pawn back and untangles. Less than you had. }) (5. Qe2 { The queen props the pawn. Playable, but she blocks your bishop. } 5... Nc6 6. Nf3 g5 { He holds the extra pawn and you are pushing uphill. }) (5. Qf3 { Also playable, and again the queen takes the knight's square. } 5... Nc6 6. Qxf4 d6 { Equal. The knight to f3 keeps far more pressure. }) 5... d6 { He hits back at your proud pawn. Build the full centre. [%cal Yd2d4] } 6. d4 { Two big pawns, two knights out, and his are both still at home. [%csl Ge5,Gd4] } (6. Qe2 { Also good, holding the pawn one more move. } 6... dxe5 7. d4 Be7 { Fine, but the pawn to d4 does the same job with more menace. }) (6. exd6 { Releasing the tension yourself and freeing his game. } 6... Bxd6 7. d4 Ne7 { He develops with tempo and you gave back the space. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop again, but the centre was the bigger prize. } 6... Nc6 7. Qe2 dxe5 { He unwinds and your grip has loosened. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
