[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The gambit is on the board, and now he must find the best answer. So must you. }
3... d5 { The book reply, and the strongest. Two pawns can capture. Which one? } 4. fxe5 { The f pawn, towards the middle, kicking the knight's support away. [%csl Ge5] } (4. exd5 { The other capture. It looks natural and it is the wrong one. } 4... Nxd5 5. fxe5 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Qh4+ { Her check arrives and your king cannot castle. }) (4. Nf3 { Developing and leaving both pawns hanging. } 4... exf4 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He is a pawn up with a free game. }) (4. d3 { Propping the e pawn. But the middle is where the game is. } 4... d4 5. Nce2 exf4 { His pawn takes the square your knight wanted. You are worse. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you had a capture to make first. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight hits the queen and you are simply worse. }) 4... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn back. Now hit the knight and look at f7. } 5. Qf3 { The Paulsen idea: the queen attacks the knight and squints at f7. [%cal Gf3f7] } (5. Nf3 { The classical road, and just as good by the numbers. } 5... Bc5 6. d4 Bb4 { Also fine. The queen move is simply sharper. }) (5. Nxe4 { Trading off your best knight for his. } 5... dxe4 6. d4 exd3 { That pawn on e4 was worth keeping him busy with. }) (5. d3 { Kicking the knight with a pawn. But look at your own pawns after. } 5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 d4 { Your pawns are wrecked before a piece has come out. }) 5... Nc6 { He develops and defends. Pile on: the knight still hangs. } 6. Bb5 { The bishop pins c6 while your queen keeps hitting the knight. [%cal Gb5c6] } (6. Nxe4 { Taking now lets him off the hook. } 6... Nd4 7. Qc3 dxe4 { His knight lands in the middle with tempo. You are worse. }) (6. Qf4 { Guarding nothing much and stepping into his pawns. } 6... g5 7. Qf3 Nb4 { His pawn kicks her and his knight jumps in. You are lost. }) (6. Qe2 { Defending the pin square, but the pressure is gone. } 6... Bf5 7. Nf3 Bc5 { He has developed everything and you have not. }) 6... Nxc3 { He trades on c3. Take back towards the middle. } 7. dxc3 { The d pawn, opening your bishop and your queen at once. [%csl Gc3] } (7. bxc3 { The other pawn. It works, but it opens nothing at all. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qf2 Qxf2+ { The queens come off and your extra pawn is gone. }) (7. Qxc3 { The queen takes, and now she is the one being chased. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qg3 Qxg3+ { Queens off again and your king still sits in the middle. }) (7. Bxc6+ { A capture first, but it helps his pawns. } 7... bxc6 8. dxc3 Qh4+ { You strengthened his centre for nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit declined"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/zBVS1QMJ"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ Some players simply refuse the gambit. You get a pleasant game for free. }
3... d6 { He props the pawn and declines. Do not grab: what does the position want? } 4. Nf3 { Develop. The tension on e5 is yours to resolve whenever you like. [%csl Gf3] } (4. fxe5 { Taking at once. But the tension was working for you. } 4... dxe5 5. Bb5+ c6 { You released it and his pieces came to life. You are worse. }) (4. Bb5+ { A check that only makes him improve his pawns. } 4... c6 5. Be2 Nbd7 { The bishop has to go back and he is comfortable. }) (4. d4 { The big centre, but his pawn takes and your queen comes out early. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains time on her. Playable, but loose. }) (4. Be2 { Solid, quiet and slightly slow. } 4... d5 5. fxe5 Nxe4 { He hits back in the middle before you are ready. }) 4... Be7 { He develops quietly. Finish your own pieces. } 5. Be2 { Modest and right: castle fast, then decide what the f pawn does. [%csl Ge2] } (5. d4 { Also good, and just as direct. } 5... exd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 { A fine game too. Castling first is simply calmer. }) (5. Bc4 { The Vienna square, but here it runs into a fork trick. } 5... Nxe4 6. Nxe4 d5 { He forks the bishop and knight and takes his piece back. }) (5. fxe5 { Releasing the tension again, and again too early. } 5... dxe5 6. Bc4 O-O { Level. You had a free hand and you gave it up. }) 5... O-O { He is safe. Do the same. } 6. O-O { Castled, more space, and the f pawn ready to open the file whenever you choose. [%cal Gf1f8] } (6. d4 { Grabbing the middle with your king still in it. } 6... exd4 7. Nxd4 c5 { He kicks the knight and you are the loose one. }) (6. fxe5 { The capture once more, and it still helps him. } 6... dxe5 7. O-O Nc6 { Level. The half open file was worth more than the trade. }) (6. d3 { Very solid, but you had a whole free move. } 6... exf4 7. Bxf4 c5 { Equal, and he chose the moment, not you. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he ignores the gambit"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/q2gn8vjV"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The gambit stands. If he simply develops instead of dealing with it, that politeness costs him dearly. }
3... Nc6 { He develops as if nothing is happening. Punish it on the spot. } 4. fxe5 { Now the capture is right: his knight has to come and get it. [%csl Ge5] } (4. Nf3 { Developing quietly and letting the moment pass. } 4... exf4 5. Bc4 Bb4 { He takes at his own convenience and you have nothing. }) (4. Bb5 { A pin, but you had a pawn to win first. } 4... exf4 5. Nf3 d5 { He grabs the pawn and hits the middle. Level at best. }) (4. d3 { Propping the pawn when you should have taken his. } 4... Bb4 5. fxe5 Nxe5 { He is comfortable and the free pawn has gone. }) (4. Nd5 { The jump, but nothing is attacking that square yet. } 4... Nxe4 5. Nf3 Nf6 { He takes a pawn in the middle and you are worse. }) 4... Nxe5 { The knight comes to the middle. Kick it. } 5. d4 { The pawn hits it, and the knight has no comfortable square. [%cal Gd4e5] } (5. Nf3 { Offering a trade instead of a kick. } 5... Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3 d5 { He trades off and equalises. The kick was much stronger. }) (5. Nge2 { Developing quietly with a knight in the middle of your board. } 5... c5 6. d4 cxd4 { He gets to keep it there. Half your edge gone. }) (5. Be2 { A modest developing move at a sharp moment. } 5... d5 6. d4 Ng6 { He gets d5 in for free and the position levels. }) (5. Bb5 { A check-looking pin that hits nothing. } 5... Ng6 6. d4 Bb4 { He unwinds and you have wasted the tempo. }) 5... Ng6 { The knight retreats to the wing. Kick the other one too. } 6. e5 { And again. His king knight has to run all the way home. [%cal Ge5f6] } (6. Nf3 { Solid development, but the second kick was free. } 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 { He gets his knight to a good square first. Much less. }) (6. Bd3 { Eyeing his knight on g6. Playable, just slower. } 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 { The same story: he plants a knight on e4. }) (6. Qd3 { The queen steps out before the pawns have done their work. } 6... Bb4 7. Ne2 O-O { He castles and you are only a little better. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop to its favourite square, one move too early. } 6... c6 7. e5 d5 { He blocks the centre and your attack slows down. }) 6... Ng8 { Both knights are back in the box. Finish the job. } 7. Nf3 { Two big pawns, every line yours, and his pieces still at home. [%csl Ge5,Gd4] } (7. h4 { The rook pawn charges. Also strong, and a little wild. } 7... d5 8. h5 N6e7 { Just as good. Developing first is the safer road. }) (7. Bd3 { A fine square for the bishop, aiming at h7. } 7... d6 8. Qe2 dxe5 { Also good. The knight simply comes with more speed. }) (7. Qe2 { The queen supports the centre. Nothing wrong with it. } 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 { Equal value. The knight is the piece that was missing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the early bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/mk3IpsGO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The bishop before the knights — not the Vienna's usual order. It looks active, and it forgets the centre. }
2... Bc5 { Out comes the bishop early. What has he not done yet? } 3. Nf3 { Simple development, with a strike in the middle coming right behind it. [%csl Gf3] } (3. f4 { The gambit, but his bishop already covers the storm. } 3... d6 4. Nf3 Nc6 { He props everything up and your edge has gone. Level. }) (3. Qh5 { The early queen raid, hitting e5 and f7 at once. } 3... d6 4. Bc4 Qe7 { Real threats, but he holds it and she gets chased. }) (3. Bc4 { Our usual square. Perfectly playable, just a shade slower. } 3... Nf6 4. Nf3 d6 { Fine, and it often transposes. The knight is quicker here. }) (3. Na4 { Hunting the bishop with a knight on the rim. } 3... Bd6 4. Nf3 Nc6 { He keeps the bishop and your knight took two moves. }) (3. g3 { The quiet fianchetto. But his bishop already owns the middle. } 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 Nc6 { He is developed, comfortable, and your edge has gone. }) 3... d6 { He props the pawn. Now break in the middle. } 4. d4 { There it is. His bishop must watch the centre fall or trade itself off. [%cal Gd4e5] } (4. Na4 { Also good: chase the bishop before you break. } 4... Nf6 5. Nxc5 dxc5 { Equally pleasant. The break is simply more direct. }) (4. Be2 { Quiet development at the moment you could strike. } 4... Nc6 5. Na4 Nge7 { Playable, but the middle was on offer. }) (4. d3 { The little push instead of the big one. } 4... Nc6 5. Na4 Bb6 { Solid and dull. His bishop keeps its diagonal. }) 4... exd4 { He takes. Recapture with the piece, not the pawn. } 5. Nxd4 { The knight lands in the middle and everything of yours is active. [%csl Gd4] } (5. b4 { A wing thrust with a pawn hanging in the centre. } 5... dxc3 6. bxc5 Nf6 { His pawn eats yours and the position is a mess. }) (5. Ne2 { Retreating instead of taking. The knight has no future there. } 5... Nc6 6. Nexd4 Nxd4 { You took a move longer and traded off anyway. }) (5. Nb5 { The knight goes hunting c7 instead of the middle. } 5... Nc6 6. Be2 Nf6 { He guards everything and your knight is offside. }) 5... Nf6 { He develops at last. Put the bishop on a working square. } 6. Bf4 { A pleasant edge: the better centre, the easier game, nothing to fear. [%csl Gd4,Gf4] } (6. Nb3 { Nudging the bishop first. Also good, and slightly better. } 6... Bb6 7. Bg5 h6 { Just as pleasant. Either bishop square is fine. }) (6. Bg5 { The pin. Equally good, and a touch more aggressive. } 6... O-O 7. Nb3 Bb4 { Also comfortable. Both bishops do a job. }) (6. Bc4 { The old square, but it stares at a defended pawn now. } 6... O-O 7. O-O Nbd7 { Equal. The bishop is doing less than it could. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the timid d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
