[Role "exercise"]

{ If he aims his bishop at your knight in this Vienna line and calls it a pin, look a little closer. }
2... Bb4 { It pretends to pin the knight. But what stands behind it? [%csl Gd2] } 3. Nd5 { Your d pawn guards the door, so the knight simply jumps out and attacks him. [%cal Gd5b4,Gd5c7] } (3. d4 { Opening the middle. Also strong, and very playable. } 3... d6 4. dxe5 Bxc3+ { A fine game too. The knight jump asks more of him. }) (3. a3 { Kicking the bishop. Playable, but he trades at once. } 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 { Your pawns double up and his bishop is gone for a knight. }) (3. Nf3 { Quiet development, ignoring the bishop entirely. } 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 { He trades and doubles your pawns for free. }) (3. Qh5 { The queen raid, hitting e5 and f7. } 3... Bxc3 4. bxc3 Qe7 { He holds it and your pawns are the ones that suffer. }) 3... Bc5 { The bishop runs to a better diagonal. Now open the game while he is behind. } 4. d4 { Strike. Whatever he takes with, his pieces end up loose in your half. [%cal Gd4e5,Gd4c5] } (4. Nxc7+ { A royal fork. But count the defenders of c7 first. } 4... Qxc7 { His queen guards it. You have given away a whole knight. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (4. b4 { Kicking the bishop with the wing pawn. Just as good. } 4... Bd6 5. Nf3 c6 { Equally pleasant. The centre push is more natural. }) (4. c3 { Building slowly. Also fine, if a little quiet. } 4... Ne7 5. d4 Nxd5 { You got d4 in anyway, and he traded off the knight. }) (4. Qg4 { The queen comes out at g7 with the knight still on d5. } 4... Bf8 5. Qg3 d6 { He defends everything and your pieces are tangled. }) 4... Bxd4 { He grabs the pawn. His bishop is now alone in your half. } 5. Nf3 { Chase it. He must spend time saving the bishop while you develop. [%csl Rd4] } (5. c3 { Kicking with the pawn, but it lets him keep the extra pawn. } 5... Bc5 6. Qh5 d6 { He keeps the extra pawn and the game is level. The knight was faster. }) (5. Qg4 { The queen again, and the bishop is still not attacked. } 5... Kf8 6. Nf3 d6 { He gives up castling and keeps the pawn. Not enough. }) (5. Ne2 { The other knight route. It attacks the bishop but blocks your own. } 5... Bc5 6. Nec3 d6 { Your pieces trip over each other and he is fine. }) (5. f4 { The gambit move with a bishop sitting in your camp. } 5... c6 6. Ne3 Nf6 { He kicks the knight and keeps everything. You are worse. }) 1-0
